  - What is our overall strategy heading into the status conference?
  - What outcome do you recommend we seek at the status conference (scheduling only, settlement conference, temporary orders)?
  - What are our strengths and weaknesses on the key legal issues (lease, notice, habitability, custody overlap)?
- Procedural & scheduling
  - What will the judge expect us to propose for discovery deadlines, motion deadlines, and trial date?
  - Are there any immediate deadlines I should know about (responses, disclosures, filings)?
  - Should we ask the judge for a settlement/mediation date?
- Evidence & documents
  - Which documents should I bring and have ready (lease, notices, rent ledger, proof of service, receipts, photos, communications)?
  - Do you want me to prepare an affidavit, financial declaration, or timelines of events?
- Witnesses & experts
  - Who should we identify as potential witnesses and when must we disclose them?
  - Do we need an expert (property condition, child welfare, financial/economic) and when would we engage them?
- Custody/financial interplay
  - How might the eviction affect the custody case or parenting-time arrangements?
  - Should we provide the court with financial documentation (pay stubs, bank statements, child support records) now or later?
- Settlement & negotiation
  - Are settlement talks worth pursuing now? What are reasonable settlement terms to propose?
  - If we want a payment plan, what should we propose and how to document it?
- Risks & next steps
  - What are the immediate risks if we do nothing or if we lose at status conference?
  - What are the likely next hearings and timelines after the status conference?
- Practical & courtroom preparation
  - Will I be required to speak at the status conference and, if so, what should I say (brief script)?
  - Do you want me to appear personally, or is it okay for you to appear on my behalf?
  - Are there any documents we must file in advance of the status conference?

3) Questions the judge is likely to ask (so you can prepare short answers)
- Who is here and who represents each party?
- What is the nature of the dispute and what issues remain unresolved?
- Are there any outstanding motions or emergency relief requested?
- Have the parties exchanged required disclosures and documents?
- What is the proposed schedule for discovery, motions, and trial?
- Have the parties tried to mediate or settle? If not, will they consider it?
- Are there safety concerns or children involved that the court should know about?
- Do any parties need temporary relief (temporary custody, injunction, or other interim relief)?

4) Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask or press on
- Are you willing to settle or accept a payment plan?
- Can you produce proof of payments, notices, or repairs?
- Are there any prior agreements or communications that resolve the issue?
- For custody-related questioning: where are the children living, and how will this eviction affect parenting time or school/care arrangements?
- Questions testing credibility: inconsistencies in dates, missed payments, or evidence gaps.

5) Documents and evidence to prepare and bring to the attorney conference / status conference
- Lease or rental agreement (signed copy)
- Any notices served (pay-or-quit, cure notices, termination letters)
- Proof of service of notices and court papers
- Rent ledger, payment history, receipts, bank statements
- Emails, texts, or letters between the parties
- Photographs or videos (property conditions, damages)
- Repair invoices, bids, or communication with landlords/tenants about repairs
- Existing court orders (custody orders, child support, restraining orders)
- ID and any documents showing current residence for children (school records, bills)
- List of witnesses with contact info and a short summary of what they will say
- Financial documentation if finances are central (pay stubs, tax returns, benefit statements)

6) Tips for how to answer questions in court (general, non-legal)
- Keep answers short and direct; say only what is asked.
- Tell the truth; don’t guess—if you don’t know, say so and offer to provide the documents.
- Avoid volunteering extra information that could complicate matters.
- If the judge asks about custody or children, explain impact succinctly and factually (dates, living situation, schooling).
- Ask your attorney before providing numbers or admission of facts that could be used against you.

7) Items to ask your attorney to prepare/document for you
- A short scripted statement for the status conference you can read or summarize (1–2 sentences of case position and what you want from the court).
- A proposed scheduling order (proposed dates for discovery cutoff, motions, trial).
- A privilege log or list of documents we will rely on and those we will withhold (if applicable).
- A checklist of discovery requests to serve and a timeline for serving them.
- A settlement proposal or mediation request ready to present.

8) Safety and practical logistics
- Confirm whether children should be brought to the court (usually not recommended unless required).
- Confirm whether you need to arrive early, dress a certain way, or bring identification.
- Ask about remote appearance options (Zoom) if travel/childcare is an issue.

9) Follow-up items you can ask me to do
- Draft an email to Sarah Chen listing these questions and asking for a prep meeting.
- Create a one-page printable checklist to bring to your meeting.
- Draft a short script for what you should say at the status conference.
- Help organize documents into a timeline or exhibit list.

