Questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask (so you can prepare answers)
- About housing/eviction:
  - Why is the eviction happening? (nonpayment, lease violation, holdover, other)
  - What notices were given (date and type of notice — pay/quit, cure, termination)?
  - What attempts have been made to cure? Any partial payments or payment plans?
  - Current living situation for you and the child(ren) if relevant (stability, alternative housing plan).
  - Ability/means to pay rent (income sources, recent changes).
- About custody/child welfare:
  - Current parenting arrangement and what you propose going forward.
  - Specific reasons you are seeking a change (or resisting one) and evidence supporting best interests of the child.
  - Child’s needs (medical, school, therapy) and how each parent meets them.
  - Allegations the other parent might raise (neglect, instability, substance use, lack of involvement) — be ready to rebut with facts and documents.
  - Any safety concerns, protective orders, or pending investigations (CPS).
- About finances/support:
  - Current income and employment status for both parents.
  - Child-related expenses and who pays them (healthcare, school, childcare).
  - Any arrears (rent, child support) and steps taken to address them.

Documents and evidence checklist to bring / have ready
- Lease or rental agreement and any notices (pay-or-quit, termination, eviction notice).
- Ledger of rent payments and bank statements showing payments (last 12–24 months if available).
- Pay stubs, tax returns, unemployment/benefits statements, employer letter.
- Communications with landlord and opposing parent: text messages, emails, written notices, repair requests.
- Court pleadings already filed, service receipts, and proof of filings.
- Parenting-specific records: school records, attendance, medical records, therapy notes, extracurricular schedules, and records showing who provides care.
- Witness list and short summary of what each witness will say (neighbors, childcare providers, teachers, doctors).
- Photos, videos, repair estimates, notices of violations, or other documentary evidence of living conditions.
- Any prior orders or agreements (temporary custody orders, prior settlements, child support orders).

Practical preparation & courtroom conduct
- Meet with Sarah in advance to role-play likely questions; practice concise answers (fact-based, not emotional).
- Don’t volunteer information beyond the question asked — short, direct answers are better.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so; ask for a moment to consult with your attorney before answering.
- Avoid arguing with opposing counsel in open court — let your attorney object or intercede.
- Bring organized copies of all documents for the judge, opposing counsel, and yourself (tabbed packet).

Questions to ask Sarah specifically (script you can use in your meeting)
- What EXACT documents do you want me to bring to the status conference?
- Which deadlines will the judge likely set and what do we need to file before then?
- Do you recommend I look for housing now or wait — how will that affect custody arguments?
- What financial documentation will most strongly support my position on rent arrears/support?
- What admissions or statements should I avoid in communications with the other parent or landlord?
- Do you recommend mediation or a parenting evaluation? If so, when should we schedule it?
- Are there temporary motions (custody or stay of eviction) we should file before the conference?
- Who should be listed as potential witnesses and what should I ask them to prepare?

Possible follow-ups and next steps
- Schedule a prep meeting with Sarah 1–2 weeks before the conference to rehearse and finalize documents.
- Confirm who will speak at the status conference (you or your attorney) and expected time allotment.
- Create a document packet for the court and one for opposing counsel per local rules.
- Start preserving all communications immediately and make backups of electronic records.

