Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is a good time to get organized. Below I’ve put together three things you can use right away:

1) likely questions opposing counsel may ask you (organized by topic)  
2) likely questions the judge may ask at the status conference  
3) specific questions to ask your attorney (Sarah Chen) and a documents checklist + prep tips

You can tell me which parts you want expanded, or I can draft an email to Sarah listing these questions or role-play practice Q&A with you.

A. Quick summary I’m using (confirm or correct any errors)
- Matter: landlord–tenant eviction in Denver County, CO (civil division).  
- Attorney: Sarah Chen, Hart & Associates.  
- Status conference set in two months.  
- Opposing party: other parent from a custody case — custody & finances may affect the eviction outcome.

B. Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask (what they may probe in discovery, settlement talks, or at hearing)
- Tenancy / lease and occupancy
  - When did you move in / out? Who signed the lease? Who lives in the unit (names, ages)?  
  - Do you have a written lease? What are the lease terms (rent amount, due date, utilities, guests)?  
  - Have you ever been asked to leave before or received prior notices?

- Rent, payments, and deposits
  - Have you paid rent when due? Show proof (rent ledger, receipts, bank transfers).  
  - What happened to the security deposit? Were deductions made? Why?  
  - Have you accepted partial payments or offered to pay late?

- Notices, communications, and service
  - Did you receive an eviction/notice to pay/quit/notice to cure? When and how (personal, mail, posted)?  
  - Can you produce texts/emails/letters between you and the landlord or property manager?  
  - Were any repair or habitability issues reported? How and when did you report them?

- Condition, repairs, and habitability
  - Are there unresolved maintenance or habitability problems (mold, leaks, heat, pests)? What did you do about them?  
  - Do you have photos, repair requests, or receipts for repairs?

- Conduct, allegations, or damages
  - Have there been complaints about noise, damage, illegal activity, or nuisance? What’s your side?  
  - Were police or emergency services ever called? Can you provide incident reports?

- Custody and children
  - Who has primary physical custody? Where do the children primarily reside?  
  - How often do children stay at the rental? Are there custody orders or temporary orders in place?  
  - Is the eviction likely to affect childcare, schooling, or the children’s welfare?

- Financial circumstances and support
  - Current employment status, income sources, benefits, child support or spousal support received/owed.  
  - Do you have bank statements, pay stubs, or proof of benefits to explain inability to pay (if that’s an issue)?

- Timeline, witnesses, and credibility
  - Exactly when did specific events happen (timeline)? Who else saw or heard relevant events?  
  - Can witnesses corroborate your account (neighbors, tradespeople, social workers, daycare)?

- Intent and settlement
  - Are you willing to move, pay, or enter mediation? What terms would you accept?  
  - Have you been offered a settlement or payment plan? Did you accept/refuse?

C. Questions the judge is likely to ask at the status conference
- Case status & schedule
  - Where do the parties stand? (Are there pending motions? completed/ongoing discovery?)  
  - What is the outstanding discovery or motion schedule? Are you ready to proceed to trial?  
  - Can this matter be resolved by agreement, or is a trial necessary? Is additional time needed?

- Relief and temporary matters
  - Are you requesting any temporary orders (possession, injunctions, stay of eviction)?  
  - Is either party seeking emergency relief or protective orders (especially given the custody overlap)?

- Custody/child impact
  - How will the eviction affect the children’s living situation, schooling, or welfare?  
  - Are there additional family-court orders the civil division should know about?

- Settlement/mediation and next steps
  - Have the parties tried mediation or settlement discussions? Are they willing to attempt mediation?  
  - Proposed schedule for discovery completion, dispositive motions, pretrial conference, and trial date.

- Documentation & evidence
  - Do the parties have evidence ready (ledgers, notices, communications, orders)? Are copies provided to the court/other side?

D. Questions to ask your attorney (Sarah Chen) — things you should get clear answers on before the status conference
Case strategy and likely outcomes
- What is our primary legal strategy for the eviction given the custody/financial overlap?  
- What defenses do we have (procedural/service defects, repairs/habitability, retalation, rent paid, improper notice)?  
- How likely is a settlement vs. trial? What are realistic outcomes?

Procedures, deadlines & logistics
- What must we file before the status conference? Any motions or declarations?  
- Are there discovery requests we should serve or expect (documents, interrogatories, depositions)? What is the timing?  
- What will you (Sarah) argue at the status conference? Do you want me to be present or speak? If so, what will I be asked to say?

Evidence & documentation
- Exactly which documents should I gather and bring (I list a checklist below)?  
- Do you need sworn declarations or exhibits prepared from me? How should they be formatted and how many copies?

Custody interplay
- How does the ongoing custody matter affect our eviction defense/relief? Should we notify family court?  
- Are there confidentiality or child-safety concerns we should raise (in-camera, redacted documents)?

Settlement and negotiation
- What settlement options should I be prepared to consider (payment plan, move-out timeline, dismissal conditions)?  
- If opposing counsel makes an offer at the status conference, can I accept or must I consult you first?

Courtroom behavior and testimony
- If I’m called to testify, what are the key points to emphasize and what to avoid saying?  
- How should I handle cross-examination about finances and custody? Any lines to avoid?

Costs & next steps
- What are expected attorney fees and court costs going forward? Can we minimize them with mediation?  
- What is our communication plan (how and when should I contact you)? Who contacts third parties (witnesses, employers)?

E. Documents checklist to assemble now
Bring originals and make copies for attorney/court:
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages) and any amendments.  
- Rent payment records: receipts, bank transfers, rent ledger, cancelled checks.  
- Copies of all notices served/received (eviction notice, cure/pay/quit). Dates and method of service.  
- All communications with landlord/property manager: emails, texts, voicemails, letters.  
- Photos/videos of unit condition and dates; repair requests and responses.  
- Receipts/invoices for repairs or services.  
- Police reports, incident reports, 911 calls, and any contact with enforcement or child protective services.  
- Court orders from custody case, parenting plans, temporary orders, and any child support orders.  
- Proof of income: paystubs, bank statements, benefit letters, tax returns.  
- Witness contact info and brief statements (neighbors, tradespeople, childcare providers).  
- Any prior settlement offers or payment plans.

F. Practical tips for answering questions and preparing for the conference
- Be truthful and precise. If you don’t know, say “I don’t recall” rather than guessing.  
- Keep answers short and directly responsive. Don’t volunteer extra information.  
- Ask for clarification if a question is ambiguous. Pause and think before answering.  
- Bring organized folders with a timeline and labeled exhibits; give copies to your attorney.  
- Discuss and rehearse key points with Sarah beforehand; she may want you to avoid testifying at the status conference unless necessary.  
- If custody or child safety is sensitive, ask your attorney about filing under seal or requesting in-camera discussion.  
- Dress conservatively, arrive early, bring ID, and have a phone number for your attorney.

G. Next actions I can do for you right now
- Draft an email to Sarah Chen listing the questions you want to ask (I can send that as a draft for your review).  
- Create a printable checklist/timeline you can bring to the conference.  
- Role-play likely Q&A (I play opposing counsel or the judge; you practice answers).  
- Search your emails (if you give permission) for lease/notice/communication to assemble evidence.

Which of those would you like me to do next? If you want, tell me whether you want the email to Sarah sent as a draft or just prepared for you to review.

