- Key issues you think matter: nonpayment/lease violations, housing stability, financial details that affect both eviction and custody, any custody orders already in place.

2) Questions to ask your attorney (Sarah Chen) — priority list
A. Case strategy & immediate steps
- What is our overall strategy for the eviction proceeding and how (if at all) should we coordinate with the custody case?
- What outcome are we seeking at the status conference (scheduling order, discovery plan, settlement conference, or next hearing)?
- What are the realistic strengths and weaknesses of our position and of the other side’s position?
- Are there any procedural defenses or motions we should consider now (e.g., improper notice, procedural defects)?

B. Evidence & discovery
- What documents/evidence will be most persuasive to the judge in the eviction case?
- What discovery should we request (rent ledgers, repair/maintenance records, communications, witnesses)?
- Should we subpoena any records (bank statements, text messages, social service records)?
- How should we preserve evidence and communications moving forward?

C. Custody interaction & confidentiality
- Could the eviction records or housing instability be used against me in the custody case? How can we minimize harmful disclosures?
- What information about the custody dispute must or should be disclosed to the civil eviction court?
- Are any court filings or exhibits sealed or protected to prevent use in the custody case?

D. Settlement & negotiation
- Should we attempt settlement/mediation before the status conference? If so, what terms should we push for?
- If the other side proposes a payment plan or move-out deadline, what is an acceptable settlement range?
- If negotiation fails, what are the next steps and what timeline should we expect?

E. Timeline & logistics for status conference
- What will happen at the status conference in Denver County (what will the judge typically ask)?
- Are there pre-conference filings we need to prepare (status reports, proposed scheduling order)?
- What evidence or documentation should I bring on the day of the conference?
- How long will the conference likely last and will I need to be prepared to speak?

F. Practical & emergency issues
- If I face immediate housing/childcare emergency, what emergency relief (temporary orders) can we seek?
- If the sheriff shows up to effectuate eviction before the status conference, what are our emergency options?

3) What the judge at a Denver County civil status conference will likely ask (and short model responses)
Note: judges vary, but these are very common status-conference topics.

Likely judge questions:
- “What is the current status of the case?” — Model response: “We are at the initial scheduling stage; discovery has not yet begun / discovery is in progress.” (Your attorney will answer these.)
- “Have the parties met and conferred about discovery or settlement?” — Model: “Yes, we have exchanged initial communications and are discussing possible settlement options” or “Not yet; we request scheduling for discovery.”
- “What steps remain before trial?” — Model: “We need document production, limited depositions, and to resolve two pending motions” (adjust to your facts).
- “Do you request a trial date, or do you prefer more time to negotiate?” — Model: “We request X months to complete discovery (or we are ready for trial).”
- “Are there any emergency matters or protective orders needed?” — Model: “Not at this time” or “Yes — we would like to address [specific urgency].”

Practice concise, factual answers; your attorney will speak for legal matters but you should be ready to confirm timeline and factual points.

