Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Below is a practical, organized set of things to ask Sarah Chen and to prepare for the status conference (and for likely questions from the judge or opposing counsel). I’ve grouped items so you can use them as a checklist in your meeting with your attorney and to rehearse short answers for court.

Important: I’m not your lawyer. Use these prompts to prepare questions and practice answers with Sarah Chen — she’ll confirm Colorado/District-specific law and strategy.

1) Questions to ask your attorney (Sarah Chen)
- Case status & deadlines
  - What are the next procedural deadlines and what exactly will happen at the status conference?
  - Are there any required filings or disclosures I should have ready before the conference?
- Strategy & likely motions
  - Do you recommend any motions or temporary orders before the status conference (e.g., temporary custody, emergency housing, stay of eviction)?
  - What is our immediate strategy: defend eviction, negotiate move-out, ask for time to find housing, pursue counterclaims?
- Evidence & discovery
  - What documents do we need to produce now (lease, notices, rental ledger, payment records, text messages, repair requests, photographs)?
  - What written discovery or subpoenas should we serve (bank records, employer income verification, school records)?
- Custody-specific questions
  - How does the eviction or housing instability affect custody claims under Colorado law?
  - Should we seek temporary custody or parenting-time orders now? What evidence supports the child’s stable residence?
- Financial questions & implications
  - How will unpaid rent, benefits, child support, or shared expenses be considered by the judge?
  - If I can’t pay rent, what documentation will help (budget, proof of income loss, applications for benefits)?
- Settlement & alternatives
  - Is mediation or a settlement conference appropriate here? If so, what settlement terms are realistic?
  - What are pros/cons of negotiating a move-out schedule vs litigating?
- Courtroom preparation
  - Can we do a pre-conference call/mock Q&A so I know how to answer judge/opposing counsel questions calmly?
  - Who will speak at the status conference (you as counsel, or do you want me to be present/available)?
- Costs & communication
  - What are the anticipated attorney fees and other costs if the case proceeds?
  - How should I handle direct communication with the other parent/landlord or opposing counsel? (Do you want me to stop all direct contact?)
- Contingency planning
  - If the judge orders a move or temporary custody change, what immediate steps should I take (housing plan, school changes, notifications)?
  - If we lose, what appeals or post-judgment remedies are available and on what timeline?

2) Documents & records to assemble now (bring copies to your attorney and the status conference if requested)
- Housing/eviction
  - Lease or rental agreement (signed pages)
  - Any notice(s) from landlord (notice to pay/quit, cure, termination)
  - Rent payment records (bank statements, canceled checks, receipts)
  - Communication with landlord or property manager (emails, texts)
  - Photographs/videos of the property condition, repairs requested and responses
  - Records of any repairs made and related invoices/estimates
- Custody & children
  - Court orders or prior custody agreements
  - School enrollment, attendance records, immunization/medical records
  - Records showing daily caregiving (daycare receipts, parent schedules)
  - Any reports or communications from CPS, therapists, or pediatrician (if relevant)
- Financial
  - Recent pay stubs, tax returns (last 2 years), bank statements
  - Proof of public benefits (SNAP, TANF, unemployment)
  - Child support payment records (received or paid)
  - Monthly budget listing rent, utilities, childcare, transportation, debts
- Witnesses & corroborating evidence
  - List of witnesses (neighbors, landlord, childcare providers, family) and short summary of what each can confirm
  - Copies of relevant texts/emails with opposing parent (co-parenting arrangements, payment discussions)

3) Questions the judge or opposing counsel are likely to ask — and short sample answers to practice
- Q: “What is the status of rent payments/arrearage?”
  - Practice: “As of [date], I owe $X. I paid $Y on [dates]. Here are the bank statements/receipts showing those payments.”
- Q: “When were you first notified of eviction/what notices did you receive?”
  - Practice: “I received a [type of notice] on [date]. I complied/did not comply because [brief reason]. Here’s a copy of the notice.”
- Q: “Have you attempted to remedy the issue or communicate with landlord?”
  - Practice: “Yes — I contacted the landlord on [dates]. I requested repairs/payment arrangements and I have copies of the messages.”
- Q: “Where will the children live if housing is lost?”
  - Practice: “My plan is [stay with family/temporary housing — provide address/contact and timeline]. I have arranged childcare at [provider], and I will keep the children in the same school if possible.”
- Q: “Is there any evidence that the child’s welfare is at risk at the current residence?”
  - Practice: “No. (Or if yes) There are concerns: [brief facts]. Here are photos/reports/documentation.”
- Q: “Why should custody remain as-is / why should it change?”
  - Practice: “Custody should remain because [focus on stability, daily caregiving, school continuity, no safety issues]. If temporary change needed, propose specific limited plan.”
- Q: “Do you have the financial ability to comply with a payment plan or relocation?”
  - Practice: “I am employed/unemployed; my monthly income is $X. I can pay $X per month toward arrears. Here are my bank statements/pay stubs.”
- Q (from opposing counsel in negotiation): “Would you accept X settlement/relocation timeline?”
  - Practice: “I’m willing to consider a reasonable plan; I’ll consult with my attorney and respond promptly.”

4) Legal issues to confirm with your attorney (ask Sarah specifically)
- Whether Colorado eviction procedure and timeline have been followed correctly by landlord (notice periods, service)
- Whether you have valid defenses (repair-and-deduct, improper notice, retaliation, lack of jurisdiction)
- How custody law in Colorado treats housing instability and what proof judges weigh for temporary custody
- Whether you should file any emergency motions (temporary custody, injunction, stay of eviction)
- Whether any communications could be used against you and how to manage ongoing contact with opposing parent or landlord
- Whether to request a joint status conference or separate hearings for eviction and custody—how these matters will be coordinated in Denver County

5) Practical prep and immediate actions (next 48–14 days)
- Organize documents into a single folder (physical and scanned copies). Create a timeline of events (date-by-date).
- Prepare a short written statement (2–3 sentences) about your position on custody and housing to use at the status conference.
- Ask Sarah for a pre-conference call and a mock Q&A rehearsal.
- If you anticipate losing housing, identify at least 2 backup housing options and document them (so you can present a plan).
- If finances are an issue, prepare a one-page budget showing income, necessary expenses, and what you can propose as a payment plan.
- Limit direct communication with opposing counsel/parent unless advised by Sarah. Save everything (texts/emails).

6) How to present yourself at the status conference
- Be concise, factual, and calm. Answer only what’s asked.
- Bring organized exhibits labeled and ready to provide to the court and opposing counsel.
- Let your attorney speak to legal arguments; you can be prepared to answer factual questions (dates, payments, living arrangements).
- If you don’t know an answer, say “I don’t recall, I will check and provide that information” rather than guessing.

7) Checklist you can hand to Sarah at your meeting
- Timeline of events (one page)
- Copies of lease, notices, payment records, communications
- Child’s school and medical records summary
- Proposed parenting plan if housing change is needed
- Proposed payment plan for rent arrears (if applicable)
- List of witnesses and their contact info
- Specific questions from section 1 to go through in the meeting

